  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "Let \\(\\angle ABC = 24^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle ABD = 20^\\circ .\\) What is the smallest possible degree measure for \\(\\angle CBD?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(12\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Both given angles share the ray \\(BA\\), so consider where the other two rays lie relative to the line through \\(B\\) and \\(A\\).\n\nIf \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) lie on the same side, the \\(20^\\circ\\) angle lies inside the \\(24^\\circ\\) angle. The angle between the rays is their difference:\n\\[\n\\angle CBD=24^\\circ-20^\\circ=4^\\circ.\n\\]\nIf the rays lie on opposite sides, their angle is the sum, \\(24^\\circ+20^\\circ=44^\\circ\\). These two cases cover the possible arrangements, so the smallest measure is \\(\\boxed{4^\\circ}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
